Objective:

 Understand and distinguish lateral movement (same privilege level, different machine) from domain escalation
 (same position, higher privilege), and enumerate attack paths using BloodHound.

Key Distinction:
  . Lateral movement: using existing credentials/tickets to access other machines at the same privilege level
  . Domain escalation: using a misconfiguration or vulnerability to gain higher privilege than you currently hold

Both are separate techniques with separate goals. Lateral movement extends access
across the network; escalation increases the privilege of that access.

 Enumeration Performed:
   . svc_backup and asrep — fully enumerated via BloodHound and netexec
   . Both confirmed as dead ends: no local admin rights anywhere, no domain group memberships beyond defaults,
     no useful ACL paths
   . BloodHound ingested and reviewed — only jdoe explored in detail at this stage

Dead End = Legitimate Finding
 A cracked account with no useful access is a real, reportable finding — it tells a client that the account exists
 with a weak password and has been assessed. It is never acceptable to manufacture a finding or hide a dead end.
 Document it and move on.

 Tools Used
 bash

 netexec smb 192.168.56.0/24 -u svc_backup -p Password123
 
 netexec smb 192.168.56.0/24 -u asrep -p Password123

  bloodhound-python -u jdoe -p 'Password123!' -d redteamlab.local \
  -dc WIN-56JEHJD0RI3.redteamlab.local -c All


Key Lessons:

  . Local admin rights and domain group membership are two independent privilege systems — check both separately
  . High-to-low control in BloodHound (Domain Admins → normal user) = normal AD hierarchy, not a finding
  . Low-to-high control = real attack path worth investigating
  . BloodHound shows relationships, not just membership — use shortest-path queries for real attack surface

Interview Explanation:

"Lateral movement lab established the distinction between moving sideways across the network at the same privilege
 level versus escalating to higher privilege. I enumerated both cracked service accounts via BloodHound and netexec,
 confirmed they were dead ends with no useful access, and documented them as findings rather than manufacturing paths that didn't exist."
